  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-67962",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To find how many  times the satellite  can go around the Earth in $1$ day, we can compute the orbital unit rate per day. We do this by dividing the amount of the orbit the satellite travels, $\\frac{1}{3},$ by the corresponding fraction of a day, $\\frac{1}{6},$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷\\frac{1}{6} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{6}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 6}{3\\times 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the satellite can orbit Earth $2$ times in $1$ day.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
